Sorry for the delay. I would do something more like the following:
```
import tensorflow.keras.backend as K

class TFModelUtils:

    def num_parameters(self) -> int:
        return self.num_non_trainable_parameters() + self.num_trainable_parameters()

    def num_non_trainable_parameters(self) -> int:
        return sum([K.count_params(w) for w in self.non_trainable_variables])

    def num_trainable_parameters(self) -> int:
        return sum([K.count_params(w) for w in self.trainable_variables])
```

Using the `count_params` function looks more elegant to me. Also, you can have simple functions to get the number of trainable and non trainable variables, but it is just my opinion your way is also ok :)

@thomwolf @LysandreJik This is mostly a refactor of the logic keeping the existing behavior of `get_from_cache`. The logic flow is easier to understand now, IMO.

The only behavior change is when etag is None, the cached file doesn't exist, AND there are no matching files on disk: we used to return the cached file (which doesn't exist). We now return None and document this behavior.

Also cc @aaugustin as he refactored this same method pretty recently.
